    "ls|LS16_S12_U_317": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *317 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 08, AUGUST, 2017 / SHRAVANA 17, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nDEVELOPMENT PROGRAMME FOR REMOTE/ INACCESSIBLE BORDER AREAS \n \n*317.  SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has  initiated any special development  \nprogramme for the people living in remote  and inaccessible areas  situated \nnear the Indo -Pak borders to provide essential infrastructure to meet any  \neventuality; and \n \n(b) if so, the salient features of the programme? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n \n(a) and (b):     A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n--2-- \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) AND (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *317 FOR ANSWER ON 08.08.2017 \n(a) and (b):   Government of India has been implementing Border Area \nDevelopment Programme (BADP) through the State Governments in 394 \nborder blocks of 111 districts in 17 border States, namely, Arunachal \nPradesh, Assam, Bihar , Gujarat, Himachal Pradesh, Jammu and Kash mir, \nManipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, Punjab, Rajasthan, Sikkim, \nTripura, Uttar Pradesh, Uttarakhand and West Bengal which includes the \nfour border States along the Indo-Pakistan border. The main objective of the \nBADP is to meet the special developme ntal needs and well being of the \npeople living in remote and inaccessible areas situated near the \ninternational border and to supplement the development of essential \ninfrastructure in the border  areas through convergence of Central/State/ \nBADP/Local schemes and participatory approach.   \n******** ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_318": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF FERTILISERS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.318* \nTO BE ANSWERED ON:  08.08.2017 \n \nDOMESTIC PRODUTION OF FERTILIZERS \n \n \n*318 SHRI V. ELUMALAI: \n  \n    \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:- \n \n(a) whether the overall domestic output/ production of fertilizers has risen in the \n country during the last three years and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether import of fertilizer has also declined during the said period and if so, \n the details thereof; \n \n (c)  whether the Government has introduced reforms from time to time to expedite \n the pace of production of fertilizers in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n and  \n \n(d)  whether the Government has given any incentive for increasing production of \n fertilizers in the country and if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS AND PARLIAMENTRY AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI ANANTH KUMAR) \n \n   \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n*********** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n-: 2 :- \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (d) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 318* FOR REPLY ON 08.08.2017 REGARDING \nDOMESTIC PRODUTION OF FERTILIZERS.  \n \n(a) :    Yes Madam, The  overall domestic output/ production of fertilizers has risen \nin the country during the last three years, these are  given below :-  \n \n                                                                                        (Figure in ‘LMT’) \n \nYear Production \n \n2014-15 \n \n385.39 \n \n2015-16 \n \n413.14 \n \n2016-17 413.99 \n \n(b) :   Urea is the only fertilizer under statutory price control and its import is restricted \nand permitted through State Trading Enterprises ( STEs), namely Metals and Mineral \nTrading Corporation of India ( MMTC), State Trading Corporation Limited ( STC) and \nIndian Potash Limited (IPL), under the Foreign Trade Policy of the Government. Urea \nfor direct agricultural use is imported on Government account to bridge the gap \nbetween assessed demand and indigenous production . Government is also importing \napproximately 20 lakh Metric Tonnes urea from Oman India Fertilizer Company ( \nOMIFCO) under Long Term Urea Off Take Agreement ( UOTA) between GOI & \nOIMIFCO. \n \n Import of fertilizers ( other than Urea) is free, commonly known as Open General \nLicense (OGL). Various compani es import these fertilizers as per their commercial \njudgement. Muriate of Potash (MOP) is the only fertilizer, demand  for which  is  fully \nmet through imports as there are no viable sources of MOP in the country. The \nproduction and import of fertilizers in comparison to last three years is as below: \n \n<Figures in LMT> \nYear Urea DAP NPK MOP# \nImport Prod. Import Prod. Import Prod. Import \n2014-15 87.49 225.85 38.53 34.44 2.91 78.32 41.97 \n2015-16 84.74 244.75 60.08 37.87 6.29 83.01 32.43 \n2016-17 54.81 242.01 43.85 43.65 5.21 79.49 37.36 \n# MOP includes industrial and agriculture uses. \n \n \n\n \n- : 3 : - \n  \n(c) :   Yes, Madam. The Government of India has notified the New Urea Policy ( NUP)-\n2015 on 25 th May, 2015 for existing 25 gas based urea units with the objectives of \nmaximizing indigenous urea production; promoting energy efficiency in urea \nproduction; and rationalizing subsidy burden on the government. NUP -2015 has led to \nadditional production of approximately 20 LMT as compared to 2014- 15, from the \nexisting gas based urea plants and the total production of urea during the year 2015-\n16 was 244.75 LMT, i.e. the highest ever urea production in the country. The \nindigenous urea production for 2016-17 was 242.01 LMT. \n \nThe Government has notified New Investment Policy -2012 on 2\nnd January, 2013 and \nits amendment on 7th October 2014 to facilitate fresh investment in urea sector and to \nmake India self -sufficient in the urea sector. Based on NIP -2012, any interested \ncompany can establish urea plants in the country. At present, Department of Fertilizers \nhas 2 active proposals, i.e. Matix Fertilizers & Chemicals Limited ((Greenfield project \nat Panagarh, West Bengal) and Chambal Fertilizers & Chemicals Limited ( B rownfield \nproject at Gadepan, Rajasthan) for setting up of urea projects. \n \nP&K Fertilizers are decontrolled, the Department of Fertilizers does not interfere \ndirectly in the production affairs of the P&K fertilizers companies in the country. \n] \n(d) :   Yes, Madam. As per para 5 of the New Urea Policy - 2015 dated, 2015, for \nproduction  of urea beyond RAC, the units are entitled for their respective variable cost \nand a uniform per MT incentive equal to the lowest of the per MT fixed costs of all the \nindigenous urea units subject to import parity price plus weighted average of other \nincidental charges which the government incurs on the imported urea.  A further \namendment was incorporated for production of urea beyond RAC during 2016-17 such \nthat units were entit led for their respective variable cost and a uniform per MT \nincentive equal to the lowest of the per MT fixed cost of all indigenous urea units \nsubject to sum of import parity price, other incidental changes which the Government \nincurs in the import of urea and weighted average of Central Government levies of \nurea paid by the urea manufacturing units.  \n \n \n******* \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_319": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*319 \nANSWERED ON 8TH AUGUST, 2017 \n \nFOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n \n*319   SHRI BADRUDDIN AJMAL \n \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of food processing industries functioning at present in the country, State -wise \nincluding Assam; \n \n(b) the number of proposals received from States and Union Territories for setting up of new  \nfood processing industries during the last three years; \n \n(c) the number of such proposals cleared/pending at present; and \n \n(d)  the number of food processing industries proposed to be set up in the public, private sectors \nand under Public Private Partnership mode in the next two years? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SMT. HARSIMRAT KAUR BADAL) \n \n(a) to (d):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n**** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFE RRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to  (d) OF LOK SAB PHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*319 FOR ANSWER ON 08.08.2017 REGARDING FOOD \nPROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(a):  As per the latest Annual Survey of Industries, 2014 -15, there are 38603 registered factories \nunder food processing sector in country including 1317 in Assam State.  State -wise number of \nregistered factories is given at Annexure-I. \n(b) & (c): The Ministry of Food Processing Industries has been implementing various Schemes for \npromotion and development of food processing sector in the country.  These Schemes are project \noriented and not State -specific.  Proposals for financial assi stance for setting up of projects under \nvarious schemes are invited through Expression of Interest (EOI) which is published in print and \nelectronic media.  \nThe Ministry had invited Expression of Interests (EOIs)/proposals for setting up of projects \nunder the Schemes of M ega Food Parks and Integrated Cold Chain and Value Addition \nInfrastructure in last three years.  The status of proposals  received and sanctioned is given at \nAnnexure-II. \n (d):  The Ministry  of Food Processing Industries has re -structured its ongoing and incorporated \nnew schemes under new Central Sector Scheme “KISAN SAMPADA YOJANA”  with following \nSchemes (i) Mega Food Parks; (ii) Integrated Cold Chain and Value Addition Infrastructure; (iii) \nCreation/Expansion of Food Processing & Preservat ion Capacities; (iv) Infrastructure for Agro -\nprocessing Clusters; (v) Creation of Backward and Forward Linkages; (vi) Food Safety and Quality \nAssurance Infrastructure; and (vii) Human Resources and Institutions.  The KISAN SAMPADA \nYOJANA has an outlay of Rs.6000 Crore for the period 2016-20 coterminous with the 14th Finance \nCommission cycle.  The n umber of new projects approved by the Government under various \nschemes of KISAN SAMPADA YOJANA in the country up to 2019-20 are as under:  \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the Scheme Number of new projects \n1. Mega Food Parks (on going) 01 \n2. Integrated Cold Chain and Value Addition Infrastructure 50 \n3. Creation/Expansion of Food Processing & Preservation \nCapacities \n400 \n4. Infrastructure for Agro-processing Clusters 100 \n5. Creation of Backward and Forward Linkages 50 \n6. Food Safety and Quality Assurance Infrastructure (FTL) 60 \n*** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.*319 FOR ANSWER ON 08 .08.2017 REGARDING FOOD \nPROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n \nState-wise number of registered factories under Food Processing Sector in the country \n \nS. \nNo. \nName of the State/UTs Number of registered units \n1 Andaman & Nicobar Islands 6 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 5833 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 28 \n4 Assam 1317 \n5 Bihar 838 \n6 Chandigarh  19 \n7 Chhattisgarh 1141 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 6 \n9 Daman & Diu 43 \n10 Delhi 165 \n11 Goa 88 \n12 Gujarat 2001 \n13 Haryana 881 \n14 Himachal Pradesh 166 \n15 Jammu & Kashmir 162 \n16 Jharkhand 219 \n17 Karnataka 2084 \n18 Kerala 1525 \n19 Madhya Pradesh 816 \n20 Maharashtra 3014 \n21 Manipur 23 \n22 Meghalaya 19 \n23 Nagaland 20 \n24 0disha 969 \n25 Pudducherry 65 \n26 Punjab 2840 \n27 Rajasthan 857 \n28 Sikkim 18 \n29 Tamil Nadu 5149 \n30 Telangana 3967 \n31 Tripura 75 \n32 Uttar Pradesh 2055 \n33 Uttarakhand 386 \n34 West Bengal 1808 \n Total 38603 \nSource: Annual Survey of Industries, 2014-15 \n \n\nANNEXURE-II \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( b) & ( c) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *319 FOR ANSWER ON 08.08.2017 REGARDING FOOD PROCESSING \nINDUSTRIES \nThe State/UT -wise status of proposals received and sanctioned under  the Scheme s of M ega \nFood Parks and Integrated Cold Chain and Value Addition Infrastructure in last three years  \n \nS. \nNo. \n \nName of the State/UT \nMega Food Parks  Integrated Cold Chain \nProjects \nNo. of \nproposals \nreceived \nNumber of \nproposals \napproved \nNo. of \nproposals \nreceived \nNumber of \nproposals \napproved \n1 Andaman & Nicobar Islands 0 0 02 1 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 11 1 30 9 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 2 0 3 1 \n4 Assam 3 0 9 0 \n5 Bihar 3 1 8 2 \n6 Chhattisgarh 3 1 5 0 \n7 Gujarat 8 1 27 13 \n8 Haryana 4 2 25 5 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 1 1 32 3 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 1 0 16 3 \n11 Jharkhand 2 1 0 0 \n12 Karnataka 6 1 19 4 \n13 Kerala 2 2 7 2 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 13 2 12 7 \n15 Maharashtra 13 2 77 25 \n16 Manipur 1 0 5 1 \n17 Mizoram 0 1 2 0 \n18 Nagaland 5 1 4 2 \n19 0disha 4 1 8 1 \n20 Pudducherry 1 0 2 0 \n21 Punjab 7 2 31 10 \n22 Rajasthan 7 0 13 6 \n23 Sikkim 1 0 1 0 \n24 Tamil Nadu 8 1 32 7 \n25 Telangana 12 4 12 2 \n26 Tripura 0 0 1 0 \n27 Uttar Pradesh 11 0 39 17 \n28 Uttarakhand 4 0 26 6 \n29 West Bengal 4 0 13 3 \n Total 137 25 461 130 \n \n**** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_320": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 320. \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 18TH JULY, 2017/ ASHADHA 27, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nCOASTAL SECURITY PLANS \n \n320.  SHRI ASADUDDIN OWAISI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether various coastal States  have submitted their Coastal Security \nPlans for approval of the Government; \n \n(b) if so, the details of the plans  approved by the Government so far, for  \neach coastal State; \n \n(c) the reasons for delay in the  approval of Plans of the remaining States;  \nand  \n \n(d) the time by which the decision on  the pending plans of the remaining \nStates is likely to be taken? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) to (d) :   Government of India is implementing the Coastal Security \nScheme (Phase-II) with effect from 01.04.2011 to 31.03.2020, based on the \nconsultation of coastal States/Union Territories.   The details of items \nsanctioned under the Coastal Security Scheme (Phase-II) are at Annexure. \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nANNEXURE \nDETAILS OF ITEMS SANCTIONED UNDER  \nTHE COASTAL SECURITY SCHEME (PHASE-II) \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of State/  UT Coastal \nPolice \nStations \nNumber  \nof jetties \nFour-\nwheelers \nMotor \nCycles \nBoats \n1 Gujarat 12 5 12 24 31 \n2 Maharashtra 7 3 7 14 14 \n3 Goa 4 2 4 8 04 \n4 Karnataka 4 2 4 8 12 \n5 Kerala 10 4 10 20 20 \n6 Tamil Nadu 30 12 30 60 20 \n7 Andhra Pradesh 15 7 15 30 30 \n8 Odisha 13 5 13 26 26 \n9 West Bengal 8 4 8 16 07 \n10 Daman & Diu  2 2 2 4 04 \n11 Puducherry 3 2 3 6 06 \n12 Lakshadweep 3 2 3 6 18 \n13 Andaman and \nNicobar Islands \n20 \n \n10 20 20 33 \n Total 131 60 131 242 225 \n       \nNote :  The Coastal Security Scheme (Phase -II) has the provision for AMC of \nboats, training, Petrol, Oil & Lubricants charges and purchase of  \nequipments and furniture for Coastal Police Stations.   \n \n***** \n�\n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_321": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 321 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 9.8.2017) \n \n  ADVERTISEMENT IN HINDI \n*321. SHRI B. VINOD KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state:  \n(a) Whether the Government has asked all Ministries and Departments to \nhave a Hindi version of every advertisement that they release either in \nEnglish or in a regional language; and    \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING  \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\n  \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 321 FOR ANSWER ON 9/8/2017  \n  \n \n \n(a) & (b) Yes, Madam. This is in view of the Government of India, Ministry of \nHome Affairs, Department of Official Language’s OM No. 20012/1/2017 - \nरा.भा. (नीति) dated 30.6.2017 which conveys the order of  Hon’ble President of \nIndia directing that the Advertisements released by \nMinistries/Departments/Offices/Enterprises in English/Regional Languages \nshall be released compulsorily in Hindi.  \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_322": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 322. \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 18TH JULY, 2017/�ASHADHA 27, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nCENSUS DATA \n \n322.  SHRI B.V. NAIK: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the new census data released recently shows that the median \nage at the time of marriage has increased  across all strata of society and \ngenders due to socio-economic changes taking place and  if so, the details \nthereof; \n \n(b) whether the data reveals that higher level of school enrolment with \noverall 90 per cent everywhere and above  80 per cent of Dalits has also \ncontributed to this welcome trend and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether according to eminent sociologists at the JNU, if this pace of \ngrowth in integration of the economy is  kept up the median age at \nmarriage for both  genders should increase by two years in  the next ten \nyears; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n \n(a): Madam, the Median age at Marriage for currently married persons in \nthe country, as calculated from Census 2001 and 2011 data, for rural and \nurban areas and for males and females have increased during the period as \ngiven below :�\nResidence 2001 2011 \n Male Female Male Female \nTotal 22.0 18.4 22.8 19.2 \nRural 21.5 18.1 22.0 18.9 \nUrban 23.9 19.3 24.5 19.9 \n�\n..2/ \n \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO.322 FOR 18.07.2017 \n \n�\n(b): The Median age at marriage, as calculated from Census 2011 data, \nshows that it is higher among persons having higher educational level. \nAmong Graduate and above, the Median age at marriage is 24.6 in \ncomparison to 19.6 for illiterates.�\n(c&d): The projection of Median age at marriage is not made by Office of \nthe Registrar General & Census Commissioner, India. \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_323": "������������������� �\n��������������������������������������������������� �\n���������������������������� �\n�\n� ��������� � �\n���������� ������������� ����\n������������������ �����������\n�\n��������������������\n�\n������������������������ �\n�\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������\n��\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������\n���� �������� ���� ����������� ��� ��������� ��� �������� �������� �������� ����� ��� ���������� ���������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������\n�\n������ �\n�\n��������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������� �\n�\n������������ �����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����� ������ �������� ����� ������� ����������� ����������� ������ ������� ��� �������������� ����� ������\n������������� ���������� ����������� ������������ ���������� ��������� ��� ������ ���������� ����\n�������������������������������������������\n�\n��� ������ �������������� �������� �������������� ���������������������� ������������������ ������ �������������� �������� �������������� ������ �������� ������������ ������ ��������������\n�����������\n���� ������ ���������� �������� �������������� ���������������������� ������������������ ���� ������������������������ ������������������������ ������ �������� �������������� ��������������\n�����������\n����� ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ��� �������������� ����������� ���� �������������� �������� ����� ������� ���� ���������\n��������������������\n���� �����������������������������������