    "ls|LS16_S12_U_416": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.: 416 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2017 \n \nPolicy on Coastal Management \n \n \n416. SHRI SANJAY DHOTRE: \n    Dr. SATYAPAL SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the salient features of the policy on coastal management intended to ensure livelihood \nsecurity to coastal communities, conserve and protect  the coastal stretches and promote \nsustainable development taking into account the danger of natural hazards in the coastal \nareas; \n(b) whether the said policy has achieved the desired results so far across the country; \n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefore along with the reaction of the \nGovernment thereto; \n(d) the salient features of the Integrated Coastal Zone Management Project along with its role \nin coastal management in the country; \n(e) the present status of demarcation of hazard line in the coastal areas of the country along \nwith the time by which such demarcation is likely to be completed; and  \n(f) the other steps taken /being by the Government in this direction? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(DR. HARSH VARDHAN) \n \n \n(a)  The Coastal Regulation Zone Notification issued under the Environment (Protection) \nAct, 1986, aims to regulate / restrict activities/ projects, setting up and expansion of industries, \noperations or processes and manufacture or handling/storing of hazardous substances etc.,  with a \nview to protecting the coastal and marine environment and ensuring livelihood security to coastal \ncommunities.  \n \nThe Notification classifies the coastal areas into four categories viz., CRZ-I which includes \nIntertidal zone and Ecologically Sensitive Areas (ESAs) such as Mangroves, coral reefs, marine \nparks, turtle nesting grounds, bird Nesting grounds etc.; CRZ-II which includes developed areas \nclose to the shoreline; CRZ-III which includes relatively undisturbed areas, rural areas etc.; and \n\nCRZ-IV which includes water area from Low Tide Line to 12 NM on seawards side & tidal \ninfluenced water bodies. \n \nDevelopmental activities are regulated in different categories of the CRZ as per laid-down \nnorms. Besides, Critically Vulnerable Coastal Areas (CVCAs) have also been outlined. Specific \nprovisions with respect to these areas seek to promote sustainable management practices.  \n \n(b) & (c) Yes, Sir. The provisions in the policy have led to conservation/ growth of critical \necosystems such as mangrove plantations, corals, shelterbelt plantation, other flora & fauna and \nhave prevented degradation of the coasts, controlled pollution, reduced impacts of natural \ndisasters and livelihood security to coastal communities, while promoting sustainable \ndevelopment. Besides, Coastal Zone Management Programme have been drawn up for each of \nthe Coastal States and UTs, for comprehensive management of coastal environment. \n \n(d)  Integrated Coastal Zone Management Project was launched with the purpose of protecting \nand conserving coastal and marine environment of the country.  The project has a National \ncomponent and State level components in three pilot States viz., Gujarat, Odisha and West \nBengal. Salient features of the project include demarcation of Hazard line, shoreline \nmanagement, demarcation of Ecologically Sensitive Areas, setting up of National Centre for \nSustainable Coastal Management (NCSCM), capacity building, sustainable coastal zone \nmanagement  approaches for identified stretches, pollution abatement, coastal erosion control \nmeasures, socio-economic development of coastal communities, conservation of natural \nresources and disaster mitigation measures.  \n \n(e)  The demarcation of Hazard line has been completed for the Western coast of the Country. \nThe demarcation of hazard line for Eastern coast is underway. Notification of Hazard line is \nsubject to necessary clearances. \n \n(f)  Government has decided to implement a National Coastal Mission as a part of National \nAction Plan on Climate Change for Sustainable Management of coastal areas and livelihood \nsecurity of coastal communities.  \n \n \n \n********** ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_417": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n417\n                     \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n8\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2017\n \n \n       \nFOODGRAIN OUTPUT\n \n \n417. \n \nSHRI RAJESH KUMAR\n \nDIWAKER:\n \nSHRI G. HARI:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether wheat, pulses and other\n \nfoodgrains output has been projected to be\n \nat \nan all time high \nduring the crop year\n \n2016\n-\n17 due to good monsoon and if so,\n \nthe details \nthereof;\n \n \n(b) \n \nthe details of actual output of\n \nthese crops in the country during the year\n \n2016\n-\n17, \ncrop\n-\nwise;\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government is\n \nconsidering to impose higher import duties\n \non wheat \nso as to discourage its import;\n \nand\n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether the country has adequate\n \nstocks of wheat to meet its any\n \nunforeseeable \nshortage in the markets in\n \nthe country?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡\nãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nS.S. \nAHLUWALIA\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \n& (b)\n:\n \nAs a result of \nnormal\n \nmonsoon and various policy initiatives taken by the \nGovernment, the \nfoodgrain\n \nproduction for the country has been estimated at record \n273.38 million tonnes\n \nduring\n \nthe year 2016\n-\n17\n \n(as per Third Advance Estimates) \nwhich is \nhigher by \n8.34\n \nmillion tonnes than the\n \nprevious record production of f\noodgrain of 265.04 \nmillion to\nnnes achieved\n \nduring 2013\n-\n14. Further, t\nhe production of rice, wheat, coarse \ncereals and pulses \nhas also been \nestimated at a new record\n \nfor 2016\n-\n17\n.\n \nThe details \nare as follows:\n \n \n \n \n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n  \n\n \n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n \n \nCrop\n \nProduction 2016\n-\n17 (million tonnes)\n \nRice\n \n109.15\n \nWheat\n \n97.44\n \nJowar\n \n4.74\n \nBajra\n \n9.86\n \nMaize\n \n26.14\n \nCoarse Cereals\n \n44.39\n \nTur\n \n4.60\n \nGram\n \n9.08\n \nTotal Pulses\n \n22.40\n \nTotal Foodgrains\n \n273.38\n \n                        \nNote: As per Third Advance Estimates for 2016\n-\n17\n \n \n \n(c): \n \nT\nhere \nis no \nproposal\n \nunder consideration to impose higher \nimport \nduty\n \non wheat.\n \n \n(d): \n \nThe stock position of wheat in the Central Pool as on 01.07.2017 is 322.75 \nlakh \ntonnes \nagainst the buffer norms of 275.80\n \nlakh tonnes as on 1\nst\n \nJuly, 2017\n.\n \nHence, the \ncountry has adequate stocks of wheat to meet any unforeseen shortage in the markets \nin the country.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n******\n ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_418": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 418 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2017 \n \nChecking of Pollution \n \n418. SHRI HARISHCHANDRA CHAVAN: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has released any fund to the State Government of Maharashtra \nto check pollution;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof during the last three years; and  \n(c)  the details of work done through the said fund during the last three years? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(DR. HARSH VARDHAN)  \n \n(a) to (c) Details of funds released to Maharashtra to check pollution and address other \nenvironmental issues under the National River Conservation Plan (NRCP), National Ambient Air \nMonitoring Programme (NAMP) and National Water Monitoring Programme (NWMP) during \nthe last three years are annexed. \n \nActivities carried out from these funds include construction of sewage treatment plants, laying of \nsewer lines, construction of sewer lines, construction of community toilets, Geographic \nInformation System (GIS), Centralised Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA) \nsystem, rehabilitation of sewerage pumping stations, land acquisition, public participation, \nawareness, monitoring of waste water quality and operation of air and water quality monitoring \nstations. \n***** \n\nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO. 418 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 18.07.2017 REGARDING \"CHECKING OF \nPOLLUTION\" RAISED BY SHRI HARISHCHANDRA CHAVAN, HON’BLE MEMBER \nOF PARLIAMENT \n \nFunds released to Maharashtra under the National River Conservation Plan (NRCP),  National \nAmbient Air Monitoring Programme (NAMP) and National Water Monitoring Programme \n(NWMP) during the last three years: \n(amount in Rs lakh) \nS. No. Programme 2014-15 2015-16  2016-17 \n1 NRCP - 499.0 2100.00 \n2 NAMP - - 163.17 \n3 NWMP - 83.20 - \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_419": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 419 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH JULY, 2017 \n \nFOOD ITEMS UNDER NFSA \n \n419.  SHRI M. CHANDRAKASI: \nADV. CHINTAMAN NAVASHA WANAGA: \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details and quantum of food items being provided to States/UTs at \nsubsidised rates for distribution to BPL families as envisaged under the National \nFood Security Act, 2013;  \n \n(b)  whether the Government has stopped supply of sugar at subsidised \nrates for distribution to BPL families through Public Distribution System in the \nStates/UTs, if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n(c)  whether the Union Government has taken a decision not to raise the \nprice of food items in Public Distribution System (PDS) shops and village level \nshops at least till the next year, if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d)  the long term policy of the Government with regard to food subsidy to \nBPL families? \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR  CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY) \n(a): Under the National Food Security Act, 2013 (NFSA), the eligible households, \nwhich comprises of households covered under  Antyodaya Anna Yojana (AAY) and \npriority households, are entitled to receive foodgrains ( rice, wheat or \ncoarsegrains or any combination thereof) at highly subsidized  prices.  There is no \nBPL category under NFSA.  \n \n          Currently 45.93 lakh tons of foodgrains per month are being allocated to the \nStates/UTs under NFSA.  \n \n(b):  The Governme nt of India has reviewed the sugar subsidy scheme and it has \nbeen decided to continue the scheme with restricted coverage of AAY \nhouseholds only, which constitute poorest of the poor, in order to give them \naccess to consumption of sugar as a source of ener gy in diet.  Such households \nare entitled to receive one kg. of sugar per family per month for which subsidy of \nRs.18.50 per kg. will be provided by the Central Government to States/UTs. \n \n(c):  Yes Madam, the highly subsidized prices of foodgrains under NFSA i.e. \nRs.1/2/3 per kg. for coarsegrains/wheat/rice have been continued upto June, 2018.  \n \n(d):  Distribution of subsidized foodgrains under Targeted Public Distribution \nSystem is now governed by the provisions of NFSA. \n \n**** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_420": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 420 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2017 \n \nGrant for Residual Treatment Plants \n \n420. SHRI ARJUN LAL MEENA: \n  \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Ministry has granted financial assistance for joint residual Treatment Plants \n(unit-5 and unit-6) under ‘‘The Policy Package for Micro, Small and Medium \nEnterprises”; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the action taken thereon?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(DR. HARSH VARDHAN) \n \n(a) & (b) The Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change does not have any \nscheme to provide financial assistance for ‘joint residual Treatment Plants’ under ‘‘The Policy \nPackage for Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises”. However, financial assistance for setting up \nof Common Effluent Treatment Plants (CETPs) has been provided under the scheme of Common \nEffluent Treatment Plants, details of which are annexed. \n***** \n\nANNEXURE  \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 420 DUE FOR \nANSWER ON 18.07.2017 REGARDING \"GRANT FOR RESIDUAL TREATMENT PLANTS\" RAISED BY SHRI \nARJUN LAL MEENA, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT \n \nS. \nNo. CETP \nFunds released \nsince 2002-03 \n(amount in ₹) \n   1 Jeedimetla CETP, Andhra Pradesh 3000000 \n2 Puddur CETP, Tamil Nadu 1050000 \n3 Panoli CETP, Gujarat 4061743 \n4 Sachin CETP, Gujarat 5000000 \n5 Sansad CETP, Gujarat 4800000 \n6 Dombivli CETP, Maharashtra 14225000 \n7 Lote Parshuram CETP, Maharashtra 10089000 \n8 Ramdaspura CETP, Punjab 700000 \n9 Veraval CETP, Gujarat 9820000 \n10 Mahad CETP, Maharashtra 18600000 \n11 Patalganga CETP, Maharashtra 17500000 \n12 Badlapur CETP, Maharashtra 9586000 \n13 Roha CETP, Maharashtra 31249990 \n14 Thane Belapur CETP, Maharashtra 20625000 \n15 Tarapur CETP, Maharashtra 41107180 \n16 Veraval CETP, Gujarat 6743756 \n17 Chikholi Morivali CETP, Maharashtra 3169023 \n18 Palsana CETP, Gujarat 40250000 \n19 Taloja CETP, Maharashtra 20240545 \n20 Dombivli Better Env. System Association  (DBESA), CETP, Maharashtra 4608000 \n21 Mallapur & Nacharam CETP, Andhra Pradesh 13200000 \n22 Waluj CETP, Maharashtra 46138000 \n23 Pandesara CETP, Gujarat 85397034 \n24 Unit V CETP, Mandya, Pali, Rajasthan 4113000 \n25 Unit VI CETP, Punayata, Pali, Gujarat 4113000 \n26 Palsana CETP upgradation, Gujarat 136600000 \n27 New Palsana CETP, Gujarat 150000000 \n28 M/s Globe Enviro Care Ltd. CETP,  Sachin, Gujarat 1791000 \n29 Kondapally CETP, Andhra Pradesh 2000000 \n30 Bahadur Ke Textile CETP, Ludhiana Punjab 15000000 \n TOTAL 724777271 \n  ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_421": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS\nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 421\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 18th July, 2017\nGeneric Medicines\n421. SHRI Y.V. SUBBA REDDY:\nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that generic medicines are 80-90% cheaper than the\nbranded and patented medicines; \n(b) if so, the reasons why the sale of generic medicines are not picking up in\nspite of so many initiatives; \n(c) whether it is a fact that people have lesser faith on generic medicines as\ncompared  to  branded  ones  and  doctors  are  also  advising  them  to  buy\nbranded ones; and \n(d)  if so, the reaction of the Government thereto  along  with  the steps\nproposed to be taken to promote generic medicines?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND\nHIGHWAYS; MINISTRY OF SHIPPING AND MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS\nAND FERTILIZERS (SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA)\n(a): The Drug (Prices Control) Order 2013 (DPCO, 2013) does not distinguish\nbetween Generic and Branded/Patented Medicines for fixing the ceiling prices\nof scheduled medicines. \n(b): In view of reply to (a) above, does not arise.\n(c) & (d): The doctors prescribe medicines based on their wisdom keeping\nthe best interest of the patients health in mind. However, the Government is\ntaking various steps for encouraging use of generic medicines. T he Medical\nCouncil  of  India  has  amended  the  Indian  Medical  Council  (Professional\nConduct, Etiquette and Ethics) Regulations, 2002 on 08.10.2016 to provide a\nnew para regarding use of generic names of drugs in prescription . The\n\nGovernment is operating a Scheme, ‘Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi\nPariyojna’  (PMBJP)  for  providing  quality  generic  medicines  at  affordable\nprices in the country. Various publicity media are being used to popularize\nthe PMBJP and for creating awareness of generic medicines.\nxxxxxx",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_422": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \n                  LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 422  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2017  \n \n \nDeath of Marine Animals \n \n422.    SHRIMATI KOTHAPALLI GEETHA: \n   \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether mass death of rare fish and other marine animals have been reported in the sea; \n(b) if so the details and the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether certain companies/organizations/persons have been responsible in this regard, if \nso, the details thereof and the action taken thereon; and \n(d) the corrective steps taken by the Government to protect the rare fish and marine animals \nin the sea? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \n(DR. HARSH VARDHAN) \n \n(a), (b) (c) and (d) The information is being collected and would be laid on the Table of the \nHouse. \n \n \n*** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_423": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 423 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.07.2017 \n  \n  \nCulling of Wild Boars \n  \n423. SHRI B. SENGUTTUVAN: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether wild boars are included in the list of endangered animals under the provisions \nof the Wild Life Protection Act; \n(b) whether the Ministry is aware of any State or UT having issued a directive for culling \nof wild boars since they destroy the crop of the farmers and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the unqualified directive for culling of wild boar would lead to indiscriminate \nhunting of wild boar for meat etc. making the species extinct;  \n(d) whether the culling of the wild boar is bound to affect the biodiversity of the region and \nif so, the details thereof, and  \n(e) whether the Ministry would take up the matter with States concerned over the aforesaid \nissue and if so, the steps being taken in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(DR. HARSH VARDHAN) \n \n(a) Wild boars are listed in Schedule-III of the Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972. \n(b) The Government is not aware of any State or UT having issued any directive for \nculling of wild boars. \n( c) Does not arise. \n(d) The culling of any wild animal including wild boar, may affect biological \nequilibrium.  \n(e) There is no such proposal. \n \n \n*** ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_424": "1 \n \nO.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 424 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18/07/2017 \n \n \nSETTING UP OF METEOROLOGICAL OFFICE \n \n \n424. SHRI AJAY MISRA TENI: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE    \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to open Meteorological office in each and every \ndistrict of the country to understand the weather trends in farming; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the total number of Krishi Vigyan Kendras attached to the Meteorological \nDepartment in the country till date and the details thereof, State-wise; and \n \n(d) the number of farmers whose mobile numbers have been direct ly linked with the \nKrishi Vigyan Kendras concerned in the country and the details thereof, State -wise, along \nwith the benefits accrued to the farmers therefrom so far? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nd`f”k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h \n(SHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA) \n \n(a) & (b): India Meteorological Department is implementing District level \nAgrometeorological Advisory Services (AAS) in collaboration with Indian Council of \nAgricultural Research (ICAR). Starting from existing 130 Agromet Field Units (AMFUs) \nat district level, AAS will be gradually increased to remaining districts of the country in a \nphased manner. \n  \n(c) 107 Krishi Vigyan Kendras are attached to the Meteorological Department in the \ncountry. The state-wise list is placed at Annexure-1.  \n \n(d) 13878276 farmers’ mobile numbers have been directly linked with the Krishi \nVigyan Kendras concerned in the country. The state -wise and district -wise details are \nplaced at Annexure-2.  The benefits accrued to the farmers are given below:- \n\n2 \n \n \n \n \n Advisories on pre -season operations like selection of good quality seed and testing \nof germination percentage which improves productivity due to good plant stand in \nthe field. \n Rain water harvesting for storage in farm ponds and efficient use through micro \nirrigation at critical crop growth stages. \n Efficient planting methods using appropriate farm machinery to minimize cost of \nlabour and increase productivity. \n Pest and disease alerts for timely management and prevention of economic crop loss \nand reduce morbidity and mortality in livestock, poultry and fisheries. \n Climate resilient interventions in agriculture and allied sectors. \n \n******** \n \n\n3 \n \nAnnexure-1 \n[Part (c) of Lok Sabha USQ No.424 for 18/07/2017] \n \nKrishi Vigyan Kendras attached to the Meteorological Department in the \ncountry(State-wise) \n \nSl. No. State Name of KVK which is linked to IMD State-wise total \nnumber \n1 Punjab Amritsar 14 \n    Bathinda   \n    Faridkot   \n    Fatehgarh Sahib   \n    Ferozepur   \n    Gurdaspur   \n    Hoshiarpur   \n    Jalandhar   \n    Moga   \n    Muktsar   \n    Nawanshahar   \n    Patiala   \n    Ropar   \n    Samrala   \n2 Himachal \nPradesh Bilaspur \n7 \n    Hamirpur   \n    Mandi   \n    Sirmaur   \n    Una   \n    Kinnaur   \n    Solan   \n3 Jammu& \nKashmir Kathua \n2 \n    Kupwara   \n4 Uttarakhand Haridwar 1 \n5 Uttar \nPradesh \nJhansi 1 \n6 Arunachal \nPradesh \nLohit, Tirap, Lower Dibang Valley 3 \n7 Assam Sibsagar, Udalguri, Baksa, Nalbari, \nGoalpara \n5 \n8 Manipur Chandel, Senapati 2 \n9 Meghalaya East Khasi Hills 1 \n10 Nagaland Wokha, Longleng 2 \n11 Mizoram Lawngtlai, Serchhip 2 \n12 Sikkim West Sikkim, South Sikkim 2 \n13 Tripura Dhalai 1 \n14 Bihar Araria 21 \n    Aurangabad   \n    Banka   \n\n4 \n \n    Begusarai   \n    Bhagalpur   \n    Bhojpur   \n    East Chamapran   \n    Jehanabad   \n    Katihar    \n    Madhepura    \n    Madhubani   \n    Munger   \n    Muzaffarpur   \n    Nalanda   \n    Saharsa   \n    Samastipur