    "ls|LS16_S12_U_425": "ORIGINAL IN HINDI \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 425 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH JULY, 2017 \n \nRATION CARDS FOR BPL PEOPLE \n \n425.  SHRI RAM TAHAL CHOUDHARY: \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the needy people and the people living below poverty line \nhave no ration cards in Jharkhand particularly in Ranchi district whereas the \npeople living there have been demanding for the same;  \n \n(b)  if so, the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(c)  the steps taken by the Government  in this regard so as to provide \nfoodgrains at concessional rates to the people living below poverty line? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR  CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY) \n (a) to (c):   The National Food Security Act (NFSA) provides for coverage \nof upto 86.48% of rural and 60.20% of urban population of Jharkhand for \nreceiving highly subsidized foodgrains which at 2011 census population \nworks out to be 264.25 lakh persons.  \n \n      After implementation of  NFSA, criteria of identification of \nbeneficiaries/households has been delinked from BPL/APL categories and \nthe beneficiaries are identified by State/UT Governments under two \nCategories namely  [i]Antyodaya Anna Yojana[AAY] Households and [ii] \nPriority Households. Eligible households are entitled to receiv e \nfoodgrains[rice, wheat or coarsegrains or any combination thereof] at \nthe rate of Rs. 3/ -, 2/- and Re 1/- per kg respectively with entitlements of \npriority household being @  5 kgs per person per month and AAY families \ngetting  @ 35 kgs per family per month. \n \n    Under the Act, it is the responsibility of the State Government to \nidentify AAY households to extent prescribed by the Central Government \nin accordance with the guidelines applicable to the Scheme and \nremaining households as priority households a s per the criteria evolved \nby them. It is therefore entirely upto the State Government to include \nany particular category of persons under priority category for receiving \nbenefits under NFSA, or otherwise. \n \n         Implementation of the Act in Jharkhand has started w.e.f. October, \n2015 and based on identification of beneficiaries reported by the State \nGovernment, 144.96 thousand tons of foodgrains per month is being \nallocated to the State Government for 263. 70 lakh persons, comprising \n37.98 lakh persons belonging to 9.17 lakh AAY households and 225.72 \nlakh persons belonging to priority households.\n \n**** ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_426": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 426 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18/07/2017 \n \nORGANIC FARMING THROUGH KVKs \n \n426. SHRI RAJESH PANDEY: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE    \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the allocation of funds by the Government to Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs), \nduring each of the last three years and the current year, State-wise; \n \n(b) whether there are any KVKs engaged in organic farming; \n \n(c) the steps taken by the Government for effective implementation and marketing of organic \nfarm produces; and \n \n(d) the status of the initiatives taken by the Government for organic farming through KVKs? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nd`f”k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h \n(SHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA) \n \n(a) The state wise details of allocation of funds to Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs) during the \nlast three years and the current year are given in Annexure. \n \n(b) & (c): The Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) has established a network of \n679 KVKs with a mandate for technology assessment and demonstration for its application and \ncapacity development. As part of their activities, the KVKs impart training to farmers in various \naspects of agriculture including organic farming practices like vermi composting, green manuring, \nproduction and use of bio - fertilizers and bio-pesticides etc. Moreover,  the Sikkim State has been \ndeclared officially as an Organic State where four KVKs namely; East Sikkim, West Sikkim, \nSouth Sikkim and North Sikkim have taken up various programmes on organic farming.   \n \nAs far as marketing of organic farm produces is conce rned,  provisions have been made in \nthe  Mission Organic Value Chain Development for North Eastern Region, as a Sub Mission of \nNational Mission for Sustainable Agriculture, under which State Lead agencies assist farmers’ and \nfarmers’ producer companies in developing their market initiatives including common packaging, \nlabeling and common brand promotion.  \n \n(d) As a new initiative 21 KVKs have conducted Skill Development Training Programmes on \norganic grower and vermi -compost producers job roles benefitting 367 rural youths during 2016 -\n17. Besides, the KVKs are involved in creating awareness among farmers to adopt organic farming \npractices for availing benefits of the Government schemes promoting organic farming. \n \n******** \n\nAnnexure \n[Part (a) of Lok Sabha USQ No.426 for 18/07/2017] \n \nState-wise and year-wise amount allocated to Krishi Vigyan Kendras during the last three \nyears and current year.                                                                                   \n   (Rs. In lakh) \nS.No State 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 \n1.   Andaman & Nicobar 208.15 288.90 380.94 649.90 \n2.  Andhra Pradesh  1255.52 1998.32 2773.96 2692.31 \n3.  Arunachal Pradesh 1560.64 1861.56 2079.40 1762.50 \n4.  Assam 2455.06 2618.96 3712.61 2965.73 \n5.  Bihar 2585.47 3303.22 4389.79 5097.69 \n6.  Chhattisgarh 1384.71 2232.70 2834.33 2929.11 \n7.  Delhi 107.10 115.65 101.59 111.26 \n8.  Goa 156.31 187.88 161.87 217.89 \n9.  Gujarat 2288.79 2532.70 2981.16 2873.19 \n10.  Haryana 1578.08 1838.07 1971.28 1995.68 \n11.  Himachal Pradesh 1009.18 1210.12 1471.68 1652.41 \n12.  Jammu & Kashmir 1512.47 1898.90 2250.55 2650.95 \n13.  Jharkhand 1414.38 1854.00 2309.47 2736.25 \n14.  Karnataka 2367.57 2733.65 3249.13 3825.12 \n15.  Kerala 1203.35 1367.92 1658.78 1551.40 \n16.  Lakshadweep 46.22 42.80 23.56 0.00 \n17.  Madhya Pradesh 3111.92 4377.02 5091.51 5204.14 \n18.  Maharashtra  3365.61 4426.68 5633.37 5829.43 \n19.  Manipur 1103.00 1294.81 1495.81 1158.04 \n20.  Meghalaya 430.00 473.22 573.80 650.16 \n21.  Mizoram 932.17 1021.20 1197.89 925.2