    "ls|LS16_S12_U_432": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 432 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH JULY, 2017 \nAAY BENEFICIARIES \n \n432.  SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of Below Poverty Line and Antyodaya Anna Yojana (AAY)  \nbeneficiaries and their entitlements under  the Public Distribution System in \nthe States of Haryana and Punjab during each of the last three years; \n \n(b)  whether the States have lifted their  full allocated quota during the \nperiod; \n \n(c)  whether the Government proposes  to increase the allocations in the  \nnear future; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR  CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY) \n(a)&(b):  Under the National Food Security Act, 2013 (NFSA), which has \nbeen adopted by all States, subsidized foodgrains at the rate of Rs. 3/2/1 for \nrice, wheat and coarse grains respectively, is provided to two categories viz. \nPriority households and those covered under Ant yodaya Anna Yojana (AAY). \nBPL category  has been delinked from NFSA. \n \n         Priority households are entitled to receive 5 kg of foodgrains per person \nper month while the AAY households receive 35 kg of foodgrains per \nhousehold per month. Government of H aryana and Government of Punjab \nadopted NFSA in September, 2013 and December, 2013 respectively. \nA statement indicating number of ration cards, allocation and off take of \nfoodgrains by State Government of Haryana and State Government of Punjab \nis attached. \n \n(c) & (d): There is no such proposal. \n**** \n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) & (b) of the LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 432 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 18.07.2017 \n    (in thousand tonnes) \n \nAAY – Antyodaya Anna Yojana \nPHH – Priority Households \n*** \nStates No. of \nRation \nCards (as \non \n08.03.2017) \n(in lakhs) \n2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \nAAY PHH Allocation Offtake Allocation Offtake Allocation Offtake \nHaryana 2.58 26.83 795.000 801.000 795.000 779.000 795.000 751.000 \nPunjab 1.22 35.14 870.120 560.000 870.120 972.000 870.120 801.000 ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_433": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †433. \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 18TH JULY, 2017/ ASHADHA 27, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nEXTORTION AND KIDNAPPING BY NAXALITES \n \n†433. DR. BANSHILAL MAHATO: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware  of incidents of the extortions and  \nkidnapping by naxalites in the naxal affected areas; \n \n(b) if so, the amount extorted and the  number of people kidnapped by  \nnaxalites during the last three years, State-wise including Chhattisgarh; \n \n(c) whether incidents of destroying  assets/infrastructure has also been \nreported from these areas; \n \n(d) if so, the details of such incidents,  losses caused and the number of \naccused persons arrested during the said period, State-wise; and \n \n(e) the corrective measures taken by the Government to stop recurrence of \nsuch cases? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) to (e): CPI (Maoist) and other LWE outfits reportedly collect illegal \nfunds through ‘levy’/extortion from road contractors, brick -kiln owners, \nillegal mining operators, contractors involved in government works and \ndevelopmental schemes, private transporters, stone  crushers, mining \ncontractors, opium cultivators, ganja smugglers, coal processing factories,  \ntendu  patta  contractors  and   labour  etc  involved in bamboo cultivation,  \n..2/ \n \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 433 FOR 18.7.2017 \n \nself-help groups in rural areas. Though an exact quantification is not \npossible, a study conducted by the Institute of Defence Studies and \nAnalysis (IDSA), Delhi in 2013, has assessed that the CPI (Maoist) party has \nbeen collecting not less than Rs.140 crores annua lly from a variety of \nsources. The State-wise details of total incidents of kidnapping by the LWE \nand resultant killings during the last three years and in the current year are \nat Annexure-1. The details of cases of extortion by LWE groups and the \nnumbers of persons  arrested/killed in the cases of abductions and \nextortions are not Centrally maintained. \n            The State-wise details of damage to economic infrastructure by the \nLeft Wing Extremists during the last three years and in the current year are \ngiven at Annexure-2. This includes attacks on mobile towers, mining  \ninfrastructure, railway properties, Government buildings, schools, roads,  \nbridges, etc. The quantum of damage to economic infrastructure and  \npersons arrested in these incidents are not Centrally maintained. ‘Police’ \nand ‘Public Order’ being State subjects, cases related to extortion , \nkidnapping and damage to property / economic infrastructure are \nregistered, investigated and prosecuted by the State Governments.  \n            The Central G overnment closely monitors the situation and has a \nmulti-pronged strategy towards combating LWE including security related \nmeasures; development related interventions; and ensuring rights and \nentitlements of local communities. \n***** \n\n \n \nANNEXURE-I \nL.S.US.Q.NO.433 FOR 18.07.2017 \n \nState-wise details of abduction including resultant killing by LWE groups during last \nthree years and in the current year \n \nState 2014 2015 2016 2017 \n(till June 30) \n Incidents Deaths Incidents Deaths Incidents Deaths Incidents Deaths \nAndhra \nPradesh \n6 2 7 4 2 1 0 0 \nBihar 19 6 16 5 18 3 4 0 \nChhattisgarh 29 13 35 19 35 13 12 4 \nJharkhand 58 15 30 19 28 9 10 0 \nKerala 0 0 1 0 0 0 0 0 \nMadhya \nPradesh \n0 0 0 0 5 2 0 0 \nMaharashtra 4 1 7 5 7 7 1 1 \nOdisha 22 11 14 8 17 8 7 3 \nTelangana 0 0 5 2 0 0 0 0 \nTotal 138 48 115 62 112 43 34 8 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nANNEXURE-II \nL.S.US.Q.NO.433 FOR 18.07.2017 \n \nState-wise details of incidents of destruction of property/infrastructure by LWE groups \nduring the last three years and in the current year \n \nState 2014 2015 2016 2017 \n(till June 30) \n Incidents Deaths Incidents Deaths Incidents Deaths Incidents Deaths \nAndhra \nPradesh \n6 2 7 4 2 1 0 0 \nBihar 19 6 16 5 18 3 4 0 \nChhattisgarh 29 13 35 19 35 13 12 4 \nJharkhand 58 15 30 19 28 9 10 0 \nKerala 0 0 1 0 0 0 0 0 \nMadhya \nPradesh \n0 0 0 0 5 2 0 0 \nMaharashtra 4 1 7 5 7 7 1 1 \nOdisha 22 11 14 8 17 8 7 3 \nTelangana 0 0 5 2 0 0 0 0 \nUttarakhand 0 0 1 8 5 7 8 2 \nTotal 138 48 115 62 112 43 34 8 \n \n \n-------- ",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_435": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS\nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 435\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 18th July, 2017\nGMP Norms\n†435. SHRIMATI RANJANBEN BHATT:\nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has given any instruction to the pharmaceutical\ncompanies to comply with Good Manufacturing Practice (GMP) norms; \n(b) if so, whether the GMP norms is not being followed so far; \n(c) if so, whether the Government proposes to take any concrete steps to\nensure its implementation; and \n(d) if so, the time by which it is likely to be implemented?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND\nHIGHWAYS; MINISTRY OF SHIPPING AND MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS\nAND FERTILIZERS (SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA)\n(a) to (d): Manufacture, sale and distribution of drugs are regulated under\nprovisions  of  Drugs  &  Cosmetics  Act,  1940  and  Rules,  1945  through  a\nsystem  of  Licencing  and  inspection.  Licence  for  manufacturing,  sale  &\ndistribution of drugs are granted by the State Licencing Authorities appointed\nby respective State Governments.  The existing regulatory provisions require\nthe drug manufacturer to comply with all the conditions of license, which\ninclude compliance with Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP) as prescribed\nunder Schedule ’M’ of the said Rules, to ensure that the drugs manufactured\nby them are of standard quality.  State Licensing Authorities are empowered\nto take action on violation of any conditions of such Licenses. \nxxxxxx",
    "ls|LS16_S12_U_436": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n436\n                     \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n8\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2017\n \n \n     \nNATIONAL AGRICULTURAL \nINSURANCE SCHEME\n \n \n436. \n \nSHRI JANARDAN SINGH\n \nSIGRIWAL:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nthe total number of farmers\n \nbenefited under the National Agricultural\n \nInsurance\n \nScheme (NAIS) in the country\n \nincluding Bihar during each of the last three\n \nyears and \nthe current year, State\n-\nwise;\n \n \n(b) \n \nthe targets fixed and achieved\n \nthereunder during the Twelfth Five Year\n \nPlan, \nState\n-\nwise including Bihar;\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether\n \nthe farmers have not been\n \nable to reap the benefits of the NAIS;\n \n \n(d) \n \nif so, the reasons therefor and the\n \nresponse of the Government thereto;\n \n \n(e) \n \nwhether the Government has taken\n \nsteps to ensure that the benefit of the above\n \nscheme reaches the farmers;\n \nand\n \n \n(f)\n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI \nPARSHOTTAM RUPALA)  \n \n \n(a)\n \nto (\nf\n)\n:\n \nState\n-\nwise details of farmers  benefitted in the country (including Bihar) \nunder National Agricultural Insurance Scheme (NAIS) during last three years of its \nimplementation are \nannexed.   \n \nAs the scheme was demand driven, no targets were \nfixed under the sc\nheme.  The NAIS was implemented by 25 States and 2 Union \nTerritories in one more seasons since its inception in Rabi 1999\n-\n2000 season.  The \nscheme was adopted by about 2712 lakh farmers during its implementation from Rabi \n1999\n-\n2000 to Rabi 2015\n-\n16 season a\nnd remained popular among farmers especially in \nthe frequent natural calamities like drought, flood etc. prone areas/States.  Claims to the \ntune of Rs. 55662 crore were reported in respect of about 842.30 lakh farmers during \nthe tenure of NAIS.  The NAIS h\nas been withdrawn after implementation of improved \nand farmer friendly Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY) from Kharif 2016 \n season.  \n\n \n \n \n    \nAnnexure\n \n                        \nDetails of farmers covered and benefited under National \nAgricultural Insurance Scheme (NAIS) during last three years\n \nS.No\n \nSTATE\n \n \nKharif 2014\n \nRabi 2014\n-\n15 \n \nKharif 2015\n \nRabi 2015\n-\n16\n \nNO. OF \nFARMERS \nCOVERED\n \nNo. Of \nFARMERS \nBENEFITTED\n \nNO. OF \nFARMERS \nCOVERED\n \nNo. Of \nFARMERS \nBENEFITTED\n \nNO. OF \nFARMERS \nCOVERED\n \nNo.\n \nOf \nFARMERS \nBENEFITTED\n \nNO. OF \nFARMERS \nCOVERED\n \nNo. Of \nFARMERS \nBENEFITTED\n \n1\n \nANDHRA \nPRADESH\n \n0\n \n0\n \n42748\n \n12491\n \n436302\n \n62593\n \n193649\n \n21223\n \n2\n \nASSAM\n \n2895\n \n57\n \n23643\n \n408\n \n2312\n \n34\n \n0\n \n0\n \n3\n \nBIHAR\n \n0\n \n0\n \n1579229\n \n887582\n \n1655219\n \n716736\n \n1436393\n \n406704\n \n4\n \nCHHATTISGARH\n \n0\n \n0\n \n94188\n \n5846\n \n1203919\n \n658508\n \n79257\n \n18874\n \n5\n \nGOA\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n6\n \nGUJARAT\n \n658908\n \n191137\n \n1948\n \n585\n \n502160\n \n361972\n \n2010\n \n1055\n \n7\n \nHARYANA\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n8\n \nHIMACHAL \nPRADESH\n \n16573\n \n272\n \n16182\n \n130\n \n16398\n \n2611\n \n9727\n \n3541\n \n9\n \nJHARKHAND\n \n0\n \n0\n \n64644\n \n5163\n \n536093\n \n390563\n \n53700\n \n4030\n \n10\n \nKARNATAKA\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n884713\n \n631807\n \n325316\n \n244436\n \n11\n \nKERALA\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n12\n \nMADHYA \nP